# A fight, run by rules. Slice one of the dungeon demo (see `ugm.dungeon`).
#
# The claim this corpus is here to test is that the mechanics and the monsters
# are the SAME kind of thing -- `causes` and `implies` over ordinary claims --
# and that what tells them apart is only which relations they name. Nothing
# below is machinery; the engine has never heard of a goblin.
#
# Three things the surface cannot do, and each is a TOOL rather than a rule,
# because each is a request answered by a function rather than by a search:
#
#     <dice>     roll(die, what)         the world, which we do not control
#     <arith>    calc(op, a, b)          there is no arithmetic in the surface
#     <compare>  beats(a, b)             ...and no ordering either
#
# ⚠ A tool PROPOSES. Every one of them lands `answered(...)`, a record, and it
# is a rule below that turns it into a claim about the fight.
#
# -- THE ROUND COUNTER IS GONE, and what replaced it is this corpus's own law --
#
# An earlier version of this corpus threaded a round integer `?r` through 65
# member positions: through the turn, the right to act, every attack, and every
# request. `docs/dungeon-feedback.md` measured it at **5 of 21 rules -- 24% of
# the corpus -- as clock scaffold**, plus a `may` token, plus `follows`/`wraps`,
# plus an `add` operator on the arithmetic tool that existed SOLELY to count
# rounds. The reply's proposal was that `at ?m` and spans would collapse it,
# since a round integer is a moment ordinal re-implemented in the corpus.
#
# ⚠⚠⚠ **That proposal is wrong, and probing it is what found the right answer.**
# A locus cannot make a request fresh, because THE READ INHERITS: deposit
# `roll(d20, hit(a, b))` at a later moment and the chain already answers `+`
# there, so the application changes nothing and quiescence drops it -- correctly.
# Measured: one ask, not three.
#
# What DOES make it fresh is the corpus's own first law (`docs/authoring.md` §0):
#
#     **an occasion is consumed, and a fact is not.**
#
# A request is a fact. Spend it -- deny the request and the answer in the same
# breath as consuming them -- and the next ask is a genuine change. Measured on
# the probe: three asks, no argument, no locus. So the round was never carrying
# the occasion; the DENIAL was missing.
#
# What the round is still for, and it is one job rather than three: it is a
# LABEL THE PLAYER UTTERS. An agent cannot utter a moment, so a declaration
# arriving from outside has to name its round in the only vocabulary a channel
# carries. It is never computed, never incremented and never compared -- and
# `add` is gone with the counting.

# -- the world ---------------------------------------------------------------

fact +monster(goblin1)
fact +monster(goblin2)

fact +present(hero)
fact +present(goblin1)
fact +present(goblin2)

fact +hp(hero, 12)
fact +hp(goblin1, 5)
fact +hp(goblin2, 5)

fact +ac(hero, 13)
fact +ac(goblin1, 11)
fact +ac(goblin2, 11)

fact +die(hero, d6)
fact +die(goblin1, d4)
fact +die(goblin2, d4)

# Initiative, as a CYCLE of ordinary facts. `follows` closes on itself, so
# there is no last actor and nothing to count: the baton coming back to the
# hero IS the new round. `wraps`, `<tick>`, `<wrap>` and the `add` operator all
# existed to notice that wrap and give it a number, and nothing ever read the
# number except the things that produced it.
fact +follows(goblin1, hero)
fact +follows(goblin2, goblin1)
fact +follows(hero, goblin2)

fact +turn(hero)
fact +may(hero)

# -- the player --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# §13's shape, unchanged: the channel says what it says, and it is a RULE that
# turns that into an intention. Delete `<trust-player>` and the declarations sit
# on the record, believed by nobody, and the hero never swings.
#
# ⚠ The round survives HERE and nowhere else, because this is the one place a
# claim crosses a boundary. Two agents cannot refer to the same moment, so a
# declaration names its round the way a person would.

rule <trust-player> = implies(
    { +says(player, declares(?act, ?r), plus) },
    { +intends(hero, ?act, ?r) } )

# ⭐ **`may` is the right to act, and acting SPENDS it.** Without it `turn` is a
# standing fact and `<hero-acts>` re-concluded the attack as fast as the
# mechanics could resolve it -- the fight ran for ever inside round one. A turn
# is an occasion, an occasion is used up, and *used up* has to be written down
# because nothing in the design consumes a premise on your behalf.
#
# ⭐ **And the intention is spent too**, which is what makes the declarations
# arrive in order. Three declarations sit on the record from the start; the
# hero consumes one per turn, and arbitration takes them in the order they were
# uttered. That is what the round argument used to do by matching, and spending
# does it without anything having to know what round it is.
rule <hero-acts> = causes(
    { +turn(hero), +may(hero), +present(hero),
      +intends(hero, attack(?d), ?r), +present(?d) },
    { -may(hero), -intends(hero, attack(?d), ?r), +attack(hero, ?d) } )

# ...and what to do when the declared target is already down. The player named
# a goblin, not a policy, so this is the corpus's judgement and not the engine's.
rule <hero-switch> = causes(
    { +turn(hero), +may(hero), +present(hero),
      +intends(hero, attack(?d), ?r), -present(?d),
      +monster(?e), +present(?e) },
    { -may(hero), -intends(hero, attack(?d), ?r), +attack(hero, ?e) } )

# ⭐ What the hero does when the player has declared nothing for this round.
# There is no negation-as-failure in an antecedent -- a `-` member matches an
# entry that was DENIED, not one that was never written -- so *the player said
# nothing* is not a premise this corpus can state. Precedence says it instead:
# both rules match, and `<hero-acts>` beating `<hero-holds>` means the standing
# policy applies exactly when no declaration did. §18's per-step `overrides` is
# the right one here, because these are rival answers to one situation.
rule <hero-holds> = causes(
    { +turn(hero), +may(hero), +present(hero),
      +monster(?e), +present(?e) },
    { -may(hero), +attack(hero, ?e) } )

fact overrides(<hero-acts>, <hero-holds>)
fact overrides(<hero-switch>, <hero-holds>)

# -- the monsters ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An NPC is not a second mechanism. It is a rule that concludes an attack, and
# it competes for the tick exactly as the mechanics do.

rule <gob-acts> = causes(
    { +turn(?x), +may(?x), +monster(?x), +present(?x), +present(hero) },
    { -may(?x), +attack(?x, hero) } )

# ⭐ Preemption, and the whole of it is one authored fact below. A goblin down
# to its last point runs rather than swings -- and `overrides` is what makes
# that DEFEAT the attack rather than merely follow it, which matters because the
# loser would otherwise re-assert on the next tick and quietly undo the winner.
rule <gob-flees> = causes(
    { +turn(?x), +may(?x), +monster(?x), +present(?x), +hp(?x, 1) },
    { -may(?x), -present(?x), +fled(?x), +done(?x) } )

fact overrides(<gob-flees>, <gob-acts>)

# -- the mechanics -----------------------------------------------------------

rule <swing> = implies(
    { +attack(?a, ?d) },
    { +roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)) } )

# ⚠ **Only the DICE are spent, and the other two tools are not.** A die is the
# world speaking, so asking twice is two questions and the second must be a
# genuine change to the chain. `beats(14, 11)` and `calc(sub, 5, 2)` are pure
# functions: the same question has the same answer for ever, so their records
# are ordinary standing facts and re-deriving one changes nothing. Spending
# those would be work with nothing to buy.
rule <check-ac> = implies(
    { +answered(<dice>, roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)), ?n), +ac(?d, ?c) },
    { +beats(?n, ?c) } )

rule <hit> = implies(
    { +attack(?a, ?d),
      +answered(<dice>, roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)), ?n),
      +ac(?d, ?c),
      +answered(<compare>, beats(?n, ?c), yes) },
    { +hits(?a, ?d) } )

rule <miss> = causes(
    { +attack(?a, ?d),
      +answered(<dice>, roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)), ?n),
      +ac(?d, ?c),
      +answered(<compare>, beats(?n, ?c), no) },
    { -attack(?a, ?d), +missed(?a, ?d), +done(?a),
      -roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)),
      -answered(<dice>, roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)), ?n) } )

rule <harm> = implies(
    { +hits(?a, ?d), +die(?a, ?dd) },
    { +roll(?dd, hurt(?a, ?d)) } )

rule <subtract> = implies(
    { +hits(?a, ?d),
      +answered(<dice>, roll(?dd, hurt(?a, ?d)), ?n),
      +hp(?d, ?h) },
    { +calc(sub, ?h, ?n) } )

# ⚠ **A functional attribute has to retract its own old value.** `hp(g1, 5)` and
# `hp(g1, 2)` are different propositions, so asserting the second leaves the
# first standing and the goblin has two hit point totals at once. There is no
# notion of a key in this design and this is what that costs: every update is a
# denial and an assertion, authored as a pair.
#
# ⚠⚠⚠ **THE FINDING OF THIS DEMO: an event has to be spent, and nothing in the
# design spends it for you.** A fact persists until something denies it, so a
# rule that models something *happening* re-matches its own trigger for ever.
# This took two rounds of the same mistake:
#
#   without `-hits`   the hit and the damage roll both still held, so 5-2 became
#                     3, and 3-2 became 1, and 1-2 became 0 -- a goblin beaten to
#                     death by one swing while every other rule starved.
#   without `-attack` `<hit>` simply re-concluded `+hits` the moment `<wound>`
#                     denied it, and the two alternated for ever. That is §18's
#                     *a contradicted expectation does not stop being re-derived*
#                     arriving from the corpus side, and precedence is no help:
#                     they are not rivals, they are a cycle.
#
# Quiescence cannot see either, because each pass genuinely says something new.
# The rule is: **whatever a rule consumes, it must deny in the same breath** --
# and since the round counter went, the two dice requests are on that list too.
# ⚠ **The to-hit roll is a PREMISE here, and it is here to be spent.** It is
# what established the hit, so `<wound>` genuinely consumes it -- and it has to
# be named in the antecedent to be denied in the consequent, because a rule may
# conclude only about variables its antecedent bound. The gate refuses the
# alternative outright, which is the one place in this rewrite where the engine
# said no and was right: an unbound `?hitroll` names no particular roll.
rule <wound> = causes(
    { +hits(?a, ?d),
      +answered(<dice>, roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)), ?hitroll),
      +answered(<dice>, roll(?dd, hurt(?a, ?d)), ?n),
      +hp(?d, ?h),
      +answered(<arith>, calc(sub, ?h, ?n), ?new) },
    { -attack(?a, ?d), -hits(?a, ?d),
      -hp(?d, ?h), +hp(?d, ?new), +done(?a),
      -roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)),
      -answered(<dice>, roll(d20, hit(?a, ?d)), ?hitroll),
      -roll(?dd, hurt(?a, ?d)),
      -answered(<dice>, roll(?dd, hurt(?a, ?d)), ?n) } )

rule <falls> = causes(
    { +hp(?x, 0), +present(?x) },
    { -present(?x), +dead(?x) } )

# -- the clock ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two rules, where there were four. `<tick>` and `<wrap>` are gone with the
# round integer they existed to compute.

rule <skip> = causes(
    { +turn(?x), +may(?x), -present(?x) },
    { -may(?x), +done(?x) } )

# The baton, and everything it carries: the turn moves, the old turn and the old
# `done` are spent, and the next actor is handed a fresh right to act. Because
# `follows` is a closed cycle, this one rule also does what `<wrap>` did -- the
# hero's turn coming round again is the next round, with nothing counted.
rule <pass> = causes(
    { +done(?x), +turn(?x), +follows(?y, ?x) },
    { -turn(?x), -done(?x), +turn(?y), +may(?y) } )

# -- the end -----------------------------------------------------------------

rule <victory> = implies(
    { -present(goblin1), -present(goblin2) },
    { +over(hero_wins) } )

rule <defeat> = implies(
    { -present(hero) },
    { +over(hero_falls) } )

# Once it is over, nobody's turn comes round again. Written as a defeat rather
# than as a condition on every acting rule: *the fight is finished* is a rival
# answer to the whole situation, which is what `overrides` is for.
# ⚠⚠⚠ **`<halt>` DENIES the right to act; it does not mark the actor done.**
# Written the obvious way -- `{ +done(?x) }`, the same thing `<skip>` writes
# for an absent combatant -- it fed `<pass>`, which moved the baton, and the two
# of them turned an empty room over for ever: **round 417**, 8,072 entries, with
# the fight correctly decided and every check about the outcome still green. The
# clock was gated on nothing because the clock is not an actor and did not look
# like one.
#
# Denying `may` stops it dead, because `<skip>` needs `may` too. Kill-probed:
# put `+done` back and `_turns_after_the_end` reports the room still turning,
# which is the only check here that can see it.
rule <halt> = causes(
    { +turn(?x), +may(?x), +over(?w) },
    { -may(?x) } )

fact overrides(<halt>, <gob-acts>)
fact overrides(<halt>, <hero-acts>)
fact overrides(<halt>, <hero-switch>)
fact overrides(<halt>, <hero-holds>)

# -- what the player declared ------------------------------------------------

say player: +declares(attack(goblin1), 1)
say player: +declares(attack(goblin1), 2)
say player: +declares(attack(goblin1), 3)
